<UpdatedLine lang={lang} />

<style>
  /* Heading size lives in base.css's `.subpage-title` now — Inspector and
     Policies already used it; this page had its own hand-copied clamp
     (2.25rem→3.25rem, not 2.125rem→3.25rem) left over from before this page
     could be touched. Dropped in favor of the shared class so all three
     subpages share one rule, per base.css's `.subpage-title` comment. */

  /* Copied from PoliciesPage.astro / InspectorPage.astro's shared rules:
     Astro scopes `style` per component, so a class used on more than one
     page needs its own copy here. */
  .back-link {
    margin: 0 0 1.5rem;
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.8125rem;
    color: var(--color-text-muted);
  }

  .back-link a {
    color: var(--color-accent);
    text-decoration: none;
  }

  .back-link a:hover {
    text-decoration: underline;
    text-underline-offset: 3px;
  }

  .internals-intro {
    max-width: 62ch;
    margin: 0 0 2.5rem;
    font-size: 1.0625rem;
    color: var(--color-text);
    text-wrap: pretty;
  }

  section {
    padding-block-end: 2.75rem;
  }

  /* Eyebrow with `//`, painted from CSS so it does NOT exist in the DOM —
     `textContent`-based extractors would otherwise read the heading as
     "//The path of a request". See HomePage.astro for the full rationale,
     and for the numbers matched against jmrp.io's `.section-title`: this
     page's `.eyebrow` was the one copy left at the old bare 0.75rem/12px,
     `font-weight: 600` and muted color from before this page could be
     touched — HomePage.astro and PoliciesPage.astro already carry the fixed
     `--text-label` (12.5px) / `--fw-medium` (500) / 0.16em / full accent
     color version. */
  .eyebrow {
    display: flex;
    gap: 0.6rem;
    align-items: center;
    margin: 0 0 1.25rem;
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--text-label);
    font-weight: var(--fw-medium);
    color: var(--color-accent);
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.16em;
  }

  .eyebrow::before {
    color: var(--color-accent);
    content: "//";
  }

  .prose-text a {
    color: var(--color-accent);
    text-decoration: none;
  }

  .prose-text a:hover {
    text-decoration: underline;
    text-underline-offset: 3px;
  }

  /* Same treatment as ClientSetup.astro's code blocks: overflow lives INSIDE
     the <pre>, not the page, because main sits in a fixed-width column and a
     long nginx line would otherwise widen it on mobile. */
  pre {
    max-width: 62ch;
    padding: 0.9rem 1rem;
    margin: 0 0 1.25rem;
    overflow-x: auto;

    /* Opaque, not `--color-bg-subtle`: that token is 5% translucent, so the
       background grid showed through the box and in light mode it stopped
       reading as a surface. Same call as jmrp.io's cards. */
    background: var(--color-bg);
    border: 1px solid var(--color-hair);
    border-radius: 6px;
  }

  pre code {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.8125rem;
    color: var(--color-text);
    white-space: pre;
  }

  /* ===== Snippet token colors ===================================================
     Hand-marked highlighting for the nginx/Lua snippet above — see the
     "SYNTAX HIGHLIGHTING" comment on `affinityTokens` in the frontmatter
     for why this is spans + classes instead of a Shiki-powered `<Code />`.
     Four categories, four EXISTING tokens, each already relied on
     elsewhere for AA text contrast in both themes: directive keywords get
     the same accent used on the nginx stage icon/label below (tying the
     snippet back to "where the decision happens"), variables get the
     teal already documented as AA text, strings get the success green,
     and the one translated comment line gets the standard muted/secondary
     text color. Unmarked tokens (braces, dots, the `ngx.md5(...)` call)
     stay the plain `pre code` color above — not every token needs its own
     hue to read clearly. */
  .snippet-kw {
    color: var(--color-accent);
  }

  .snippet-var {
    color: var(--teal-text);
  }

  .snippet-str {
    color: var(--color-success);
  }

  .snippet-com {
    color: var(--color-text-muted);
  }

  /* ===== Request-path diagram =================================================
     A compact horizontal strip (client → Cloudflare → nginx → one of N nodes
     → egress → destination — the six stages themselves never move, resize,
     or reorder) plus six numbered bubbles that cycle one at a time below
     it. See this file's header comment for why: two earlier versions were
     rejected, the second one specifically for still reading as "an
     animation of dots travelling" instead of an infographic, and reserves a
     fixed height for the bubble zone so the page never jumps.

     ONE thing does move now, deliberately: a single highlight indicator
     (`.mcp-box-fill`/`.mcp-box-border`, see the "Traveling highlight box"
     section below) glides between all six stages the bubbles narrate. That
     is not the same defect as "dots travelling" above — there is one
     indicator, not several, it follows the bubbles' reading order rather
     than inventing its own, and the stages/labels/nodes it glides past are
     still fixed HTML in a fixed grid. See this file's header comment,
     "UPDATE 2026-08-23" and "UPDATE 2026-08-23 (destination hop)", for why
     this changed from the original highlight-only version and for why it
     now reaches a sixth stage. */

  /* `--wire-*`: the three transport colours, defined once for the connector
     line and its legend. Site tokens, not new hex — `success` for a hop
     encrypted with TLS, `info` for the WireGuard tunnel, and the muted text
     colour for the hop that never touches a network (the same neutral this
     strip already uses for structure rather than status). Both themes ship
     AA-contrast values for all three. */
  .mcp-flow-figure {
    /* The shared icon track, in px. Also the SVG's viewBox height, set from
       `TRACK_H` in the frontmatter — the two MUST stay equal or the curves
       stop landing on the icons. */
    --wire-tls: var(--color-success);
    --wire-wg: var(--color-info);
    --wire-local: var(--color-text-muted);

    max-width: 62ch;
    padding: 1rem;
    margin: 1.75rem 0;

    /* Opaque, not `--color-bg-subtle`: that token is 5% translucent, so the
       background grid showed through the box and in light mode it stopped
       reading as a surface. Same call as jmrp.io's cards. */
    background: var(--color-bg-secondary);
    border: 1px solid var(--color-hair);
    border-radius: var(--radius-md);
  }

  /* ===== The strip =============================================================
     Six stages on a shared 8-unit grid: client/Cloudflare/nginx/egress/
     destination get 1 column each, nodes gets 3 (it holds a small cluster of
     badges, not one icon). `min-width: 0` on `.stage` is load-bearing — a
     CSS grid track otherwise refuses to shrink a cell below its content's
     natural (unwrapped) width, which for a word like "Cloudflare" or
     "destination" would force the whole strip wider than the viewport at
     360px. With `min-width: 0` plus `overflow-wrap: anywhere` on the label
     below, the grid track wins instead and the word wraps — this is what
     keeps the strip horizontal with zero horizontal scroll at 360px, the
     hard requirement that sank neither, but almost sank, both earlier
     attempts. */

  /* ===== Proportional scale ===================================================
     The figure is DESIGNED at 380.44px wide (the strip at a 440px viewport,
     measured in the browser) and scales from there as one block: `--u` is one
     design pixel, and every piece of geometry below is a multiple of it.

     The reason is not aesthetic. With fixed px sizes the columns narrow as the
     width drops but the labels do not, so "Cloudflare" and "destination" — the
     two long words — end up breaking, and every column split has to be checked
     again at 360, 390 and 440. Scaling keeps the proportions identical at any
     width and NOTHING can break: the figure only gets smaller or larger.

     `container-type: inline-size` goes on the wrapper and not here because
     `cq*` units resolve against an ANCESTOR container: an element is not its
     own container, and declaring it here would resolve them against the
     viewport.

     `.mcp-scale`'s cap exists because without it the figure would grow with
     the text column: at 1280px it would be nearly three times its design
     size. */
  .mcp-scale {
    max-width: 46rem;
    container-type: inline-size;
  }

  .mcp-strip {
    --u: calc(100cqw / 380.44);
    --track-h: calc(74 * var(--u));

    position: relative;
    display: grid;

    /* Cloudflare/destination get a bit more than client/nginx/egress: they
       are the longest words on the strip ("Cloudflare", "destination") and
       would otherwise wrap ugly mid-word even with `overflow-wrap: anywhere`
       below. Nodes still gets by far the most room — see this block's
       header comment. */

    /* The nodes column no longer holds a row of badges (they stack now), so
       it needs no more room than any other; the width goes instead to the two
       longest words, which used to break mid-word at 390px. */
    grid-template-columns: 0.65fr 1.5fr 0.9fr 1.225fr 1.225fr 1.5fr;
    column-gap: calc(2 * var(--u));
    align-items: start;

    /* Gives `.mcp-box-fill`'s negative `z-index` (below) somewhere to land:
       without this, a negative `z-index` on a descendant escapes past
       `.mcp-strip` itself and paints behind `.mcp-flow-figure`'s own
       background instead of just behind the stage icons/labels — see the
       "Traveling highlight box" section below. */
    isolation: isolate;
  }

  /* The connecting line, painted BEHIND every stage: a `::before` pseudo-
     element is always painted before its element's real children, so the
     opaque icon/badge circles sitting on top of it read as beads on a wire
     with no z-index bookkeeping needed. */

  /* The connectors. Absolutely positioned over the icon track — NOT over the
     whole strip — so the SVG's y axis and the track's px height are the same
     number, which is what lets the frontmatter place a curve's endpoint on a
     node without measuring anything at runtime.

     Behind every stage (`z-index: -1`, with `isolation: isolate` on the strip
     above): the icons are opaque, so the wires disappear under them and the
     stages read as beads threaded on the lines. */
  .mcp-wires {
    position: absolute;
    top: calc(
      3.2 * var(--u)
    ); /* the stage's own padding: aligns the track with the icons */

    left: 0;
    z-index: -1;
    width: 100%;
    height: var(--track-h);
  }

  .wire {
    fill: none;
    stroke-width: calc(2 * var(--u));
    stroke-linecap: round;
  }

  .wire--tls {
    stroke: var(--wire-tls);
  }

  .wire--local {
    stroke: var(--wire-local);
  }

  .wire--wg {
    stroke: var(--wire-wg);
  }

  /* The current. A dash of length 0 with a round cap paints a dot, so this is
     a line of dots marching along the wire — the animation runs from the
     first frame, which is what tells a visitor the figure is live before the
     highlight box has moved anywhere. `stroke-dashoffset` is the only thing
     animated: the geometry never changes, so no layout is involved. */
  .flow {
    fill: none;
    stroke: var(--color-accent);
    stroke-width: calc(3 * var(--u));
    stroke-linecap: round;
    stroke-dasharray: 0 16;
    animation: flow-dots 1.6s linear infinite;
  }

  @keyframes flow-dots {
    to {
      stroke-dashoffset: -16;
    }
  }

  /* ===== Transport legend ======================================================
     Three swatches in a row, above the bubbles. The swatch colours are the
     line's own, defined ONCE as `--wire-*` on the figure and consumed by
     both — a legend that can drift from the thing it explains is worse than
     no legend. Colour never carries the meaning alone: each swatch is
     followed by its word, and the `wire*` section below the figure says all
     of it again in prose, which is what a screen reader or a crawler reads.

     This replaced a label-per-joint version (five words riding on the wire).
     It read fine from 600px up and collided with an icon at every hop below
     481px — the gap between two adjacent stage icons is ~30px there and a
     legible label ~27px wide — so the author asked for colour plus one
     horizontal legend instead. */
  .mcp-legend {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 0.25rem 1rem;
    justify-content: center;
    padding: 0;
    margin: 0.9rem 0 0;
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.65rem;
    color: var(--color-text-muted);
    list-style: none;
  }

  .mcp-legend li {
    display: inline-flex;
    gap: 0.35rem;
    align-items: center;
    white-space: nowrap; /* each pair wraps as a unit, never mid-word */
  }

  .mcp-legend-swatch {
    width: 1.1rem;
    height: 2px;
    border-radius: 1px;
  }

  .mcp-legend-swatch--tls {
    background: var(--wire-tls);
  }

  .mcp-legend-swatch--local {
    background: var(--wire-local);
  }

  .mcp-legend-swatch--wg {
    background: var(--wire-wg);
  }

  .mcp-legend-note {
    display: block;
    margin-top: 0.2rem;
  }

  /* Two rows: a track every column shares, and the label under it. The track
     is what makes the fan drawable — the SVG's y coordinates are px inside
     exactly this height, so a single icon sits at its middle while three
     stacked nodes spread across it, and both agree with the curves painted
     behind them. `place-items: center` centres whatever the first row holds,
     one icon or a stack of them. */
  .stage {
    position: relative;
    display: grid;
    grid-template-rows: var(--track-h) auto;
    place-items: center;
    min-width: 0; /* see this block's header comment */
    padding: calc(3.2 * var(--u));
    border-radius: var(--radius-md);
  }

  /* ===== Traveling highlight box ===============================================
     ONE indicator that glides along the strip, dwelling at whichever stage
     the current bubble narrates and travelling to the next with an
     accelerate/cruise/decelerate profile (`box-travel` below, shared with
     the bubbles' timeline — see "Bubble cycle" further down). This replaced
     an earlier version that highlighted each stage with its own opacity-only
     `::after`, popping in at one stage and out at the next — the author read
     that as choppy and asked for an indicator that is SEEN TO
     MOVE. That is still the rule between stages: it never pops from one to
     the next.

     The wrap is the deliberate exception. Between 81% and 84% it fades out
     where it stands, jumps back to stage 1 while fully transparent, and
     fades in from 97% — in step with the bubbles, whose sixth fades out at
     84% and whose first fades in at 97%, so the box and its caption always
     arrive together. Before this it crawled the whole width backwards to
     restart, which narrated nothing (no bubble accompanies that leg, and no
     request travels from its destination back to the client) and read as a
     seventh step that does not exist. See this file's header comment for the
     fuller history.

     Two elements, not one — `.mcp-box-fill` and `.mcp-box-border` — because
     CSS cannot give `z-index` to only half of a single element's own box
     (its background, specifically). Splitting it in two is the author's own
     fix for that: a fill-only copy BEHIND the strip's real content (icons,
     labels, node badges — via `isolation: isolate` on `.mcp-strip` above
     plus this element's own negative `z-index`, so the fill can never sit
     on top of a stage's text) and a border-only copy ABOVE everything (a
     positive `z-index`). Both run the exact same `box-travel` animation, so
     they move as a single object with no separate clock to drift out of
     sync.

     `left`/`width` are animated (see `box-travel`); `top`/`height` are NOT
     — every stage's own content height differs ("nodes" is tallest at every
     breakpoint: 52.77px measured at ≥901px, 49.39px at 481–900px, 45.84px
     at ≤480px), and one element cannot re-measure six different DOM boxes
     in pure CSS. Sized generously to the tallest stage at each breakpoint
     instead, so the indicator reads as one consistent "spotlight" gliding
     under icon+label alike rather than tracing each stage's exact outline. */
  .mcp-box-fill,
  .mcp-box-border {
    position: absolute;
    top: calc(-4 * var(--u));

    /* The shared track plus the label under it, plus 4px of air each side —
       every column is the same height now, so one value fits all six. */
    height: calc(var(--track-h) + calc(30 * var(--u)));
    pointer-events: none;
    border-radius: var(--radius-md);

    /* `linear` here is a harmless default: every dwell segment below holds
       its value constant (no interpolation needed) and every travel segment
       sets its OWN `animation-timing-function` per keyframe — see
       `box-travel`'s header comment. */
    animation: box-travel 30s linear infinite;
  }

  .mcp-box-fill {
    z-index: -1; /* behind every stage's static (non-positioned) content */
    background: var(--accent-dim);
  }

  .mcp-box-border {
    z-index: 2; /* above the connecting line and every stage/node badge */
    border: 1px solid var(--color-accent);
  }

  .stage-icon {
    box-sizing: border-box;
    width: calc(18 * var(--u));
    height: calc(18 * var(--u));
    background: var(--color-bg-secondary);
    border: calc(1.5 * var(--u)) solid var(--color-border);
    border-radius: 50%;
  }

  /* nginx: where the decision happens, drawn "in focus" even at rest — a
     thicker accent ring and a tinted fill, not just a highlight during its
     own bubble's window. */

  /* The destination is not a host: it is whatever each caller pointed its
     client at, which is why the exits enter it on separate straight lines
     instead of meeting at a bead. A tall block reads as a region the way a
     circle cannot — and, being opaque like every other icon, it also masks
     the wire ends that stop behind it. */
  .stage-icon--zone {
    width: calc(14 * var(--u));
    height: calc(var(--track-h) - calc(8 * var(--u)));
    border-radius: var(--radius-md);
  }

  .stage-icon--nginx {
    /* A solid fill, not the tint it used to have: this is the one piece of the
       drawing that DECIDES anything, and at 18px a ring with a translucent
       fill reads like any other bead on the wire. */
    background: var(--color-accent);
    border-color: var(--color-accent);
    border-width: calc(2.5 * var(--u));
  }

  /* Cloudflare: the one hop this operator does not run, in its brand orange so
     it is obvious at a glance where our own control ends. A solid disc with the
     mark knocked out in white, rather than an orange glyph over the page
     background: at 18px a thin monochrome logo falls below the 3:1 graphics
     contrast in light mode, while the disc clears it comfortably in both. */
  .stage-icon--cloudflare {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    color: #fff;
    background: #f6821f;
    border-color: #f6821f;
  }

  .stage-icon--cloudflare > span {
    width: calc(11 * var(--u));
    height: calc(11 * var(--u));
  }

  /* The exits, inside a single frame: one bead per node, each on its node's
     row, so every node's wire lands on its own exit and every exit leaves on
     its own line. Opaque on purpose, like the other icons: it covers the ends
     of the wires that die behind it. */
  .egress-zone {
    box-sizing: border-box;
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    height: calc(var(--track-h) - calc(4 * var(--u)));
    padding: 0 calc(6 * var(--u));
    background: var(--color-bg-secondary);
    border: calc(1.5 * var(--u)) solid var(--color-border);
    border-radius: var(--radius-md);
  }

  /* The node beads' size, and for a reason: three of them plus two gaps fill
     the frame's height exactly (18 + 8 + 18 + 8 + 18 = 70 units), and each one
     sits on the same row as the node it belongs to. They carry the exit's
     country code inside — real data from `topology.json`, not decoration.
     KEEP-IN-SYNC with `EGRESS_GAP` in the frontmatter (18 + 8 = 26). */
  .stage-icon--egress {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    width: calc(18 * var(--u));
    height: calc(18 * var(--u));
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: calc(7 * var(--u));
    font-weight: var(--fw-semibold);
    color: var(--color-text);
  }

  /* The Spanish zone: nginx and the instances, that is columns 3 and 4 of
     `grid-template-columns`.

     GENERATED alongside `COL_X`, the bubbles' `--pos` and box-travel's stops:
     `left` is column 3's left edge and `width` the sum of the two, both as a
     percentage of the total `fr`. It was left out of the generator in the first
     split and ended up five points to the right of what it encloses — shifted,
     not broken, which is exactly the kind of defect nobody catches in a code
     review; hence it now comes from the same source as everything else.

     It sits BEHIND the wires (`z-index: -2`) so it cuts no line. */
  .mcp-region {
    position: absolute;
    top: calc(3.2 * var(--u));
    left: 30.714%;
    z-index: -2;
    box-sizing: border-box;
    width: 30.357%;
    height: var(--track-h);

    /* Dashed and with NO fill, unlike the egress frame, which is an opaque box:
       both are rectangles and they say different things — this one marks WHERE
       its contents are, that one groups the exits. With a fill the two read the
       same, and it also muddied the highlight box passing over. */
    border: calc(1 * var(--u)) dashed var(--color-text-muted);
    border-radius: var(--radius-md);
    opacity: 55%;
  }

  .mcp-region-tag {
    position: absolute;
    top: calc(2 * var(--u));
    left: calc(5 * var(--u));
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: calc(8 * var(--u));
    line-height: 1;
    color: var(--color-text-muted);
  }

  /* Client: three small dots instead of a ring — "you", not a server. */

  /* The client keeps its three-dot glyph but wears the same bead as every
     other stage: since the connectors became 2px coloured wires drawn behind
     the strip, a borderless icon let the wire run BETWEEN the dots, which
     read as a broken glyph rather than as a device sending a request. The
     circle's own background masks the wire, exactly as it does for the rest. */
  .stage-icon--client {
    display: flex;
    gap: calc(2 * var(--u));
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
  }

  .stage-icon--client span {
    width: calc(3 * var(--u));
    height: calc(3 * var(--u));
    background: var(--color-text-muted);
    border-radius: 50%;
  }

  .stage-label {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: calc(9.92 * var(--u));
    line-height: 1.15;
    color: var(--color-text-muted);
    text-align: center;
    hyphens: auto; /* "destination" breaks as "desti-nation", not "destinatio-n", if it still has to wrap at all */
    overflow-wrap: anywhere; /* see this block's header comment */
  }

  .stage--nginx .stage-label {
    font-weight: var(--fw-semibold);
    color: var(--color-text);
  }

  /* ===== Node cluster ==========================================================
     The pool of available nodes, all of them, always visible, never colored
     by which bubble is showing — bubble 4 has the traveling highlight box
     grow to cover the whole cluster (see "Traveling highlight box" and
     `box-travel` above), never a single badge. Numbers/countries are real
     HTML text, not SVG: this is real data from `topology.ts`, not
     decoration. */

  /* Nodes stack: one circle per instance, spread over the shared track so each
     one lines up with the curve that reaches it. `justify-content: space-*` is
     deliberately NOT used — the frontmatter computes the same even spread for
     the SVG, and two independent distributions would drift apart. */
  .node-cluster {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: space-around;
    height: var(--track-h);
  }

  /* The exits: one per node, centred as a group, each on its node's row. The
     `gap` is half of `EGRESS_GAP` in the frontmatter — see the KEEP-IN-SYNC
     note there and on `.stage-icon--egress`. */
  .egress-stack {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: calc(8 * var(--u));
    align-items: center;
  }

  .node-badge {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    align-items: center;
  }

  .node-badge-num {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    width: calc(18 * var(--u));
    height: calc(18 * var(--u));
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: calc(8.8 * var(--u));
    font-weight: var(--fw-semibold);
    color: var(--color-text);
    background: var(--color-bg-secondary);
    border: calc(1.5 * var(--u)) solid var(--color-border);
    border-radius: 50%;
  }

  /* ===== Shared column positions ===============================================
     The six bubble targets, as center-of-column percentages of the strip's
     8.6-unit grid above (1fr client, 1.3fr Cloudflare, 1fr nginx, 3fr nodes,
     1fr egress, 1.3fr destination — every stage now has a bubble). Column
     gap is 2px, imperceptible next to the figure's own width, so ignoring
     it here is fine. Each `--pos` positions that bubble's leader line and
     its box's speech-bubble tail below — one shared value instead of
     repeating the same percentage three times per bubble. */
  .bubble--1 {
    --pos: 4.643%;
  }

  .bubble--2 {
    --pos: 20%;
  }

  .bubble--3 {
    --pos: 37.143%;
  }

  .bubble--4 {
    --pos: 52.321%;
  }

  .bubble--5 {
    --pos: 69.821%;
  }

  .bubble--6 {
    --pos: 89.286%;
  }

  /* ===== The fixed-height bubble zone ==========================================
     `height`, not `min-height`: with every `.bubble` absolutely positioned
     (below), the container has no in-flow content to size itself from, so it
     would collapse to 0 without an explicit height. This value is tuned
     against the longest bubble text in EITHER language at 360px wide, the
     narrowest this site supports — see the SDD report for the rendered
     measurements taken across the animation loop. A FIXED reservation,
     never a per-bubble one, is what actually fixes the defect that sank the
     previous attempt: the page must not move as the bubbles cycle. */
  .mcp-bubbles {
    position: relative;
    height: 5rem;
    margin-top: 0.9rem;
  }

  .bubble {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    opacity: 0%;
  }

  /* stylelint-disable no-duplicate-selectors -- These six selectors appear
     twice on purpose, and merging them is the worse read: the block above
     carries `--pos`, the column geometry, under the comment that derives it
     from the grid; this one carries only the phase each bubble animates on.
     One concern per block, each next to the note that explains it. */
  .bubble--1 {
    animation: phase-1 30s var(--ease-in-out) infinite;
  }

  .bubble--2 {
    animation: phase-2 30s var(--ease-in-out) infinite;
  }

  .bubble--3 {
    animation: phase-3 30s var(--ease-in-out) infinite;
  }

  .bubble--4 {
    animation: phase-4 30s var(--ease-in-out) infinite;
  }

  .bubble--5 {
    animation: phase-5 30s var(--ease-in-out) infinite;
  }

  .bubble--6 {
    animation: phase-6 30s var(--ease-in-out) infinite;
  }
  /* stylelint-enable no-duplicate-selectors */

  /* The thin line that "points at the piece it describes" — from the top of
     this fixed zone (where the strip ends) down to the bubble's tail. */
  .bubble-leader {
    position: absolute;
    top: 0;
    left: var(--pos);
    width: 1px;
    height: 0.9rem;
    background: var(--color-border);
  }

  /* `display: flex` is the vertical-centering fix: the number badge is
     `1.1rem` tall, which happens to be close to ONE line of the 0.8125rem/
     1.35 body text — so a one-line bubble already looked fine, but a
     two-line bubble did not. Without flex, `.bubble-num` is an inline box
     that sits next to the FIRST line only; the second line wraps back to
     the box's left edge underneath it, reading as the number pinned to the
     top of a taller block instead of centered against it. `align-items:
     center` on the row centers the badge against the text's full height
     (one line or two, EN or ES — see `.bubble-num`/`.bubble-text` below)
     instead of just its first line. */
  .bubble-box {
    position: relative;
    display: flex;
    gap: 0.4rem;
    align-items: center;
    padding: 0.5rem 0.75rem;
    margin: 0.9rem 0 0;
    font-size: 0.8125rem;
    line-height: 1.35;
    color: var(--color-text);
    text-wrap: pretty;
    background: var(--color-bg-secondary);
    border: 1px solid var(--color-border);
    border-radius: var(--radius-md);
  }

  /* Speech-bubble tail: two stacked triangles (border color, then background
     color one pixel smaller) fake a 1px outline where the tail meets the
     leader line above. */
  .bubble-box::before,
  .bubble-box::after {
    position: absolute;
    top: -7px;
    left: var(--pos);
    content: "";
    border-right: 7px solid transparent;
    border-left: 7px solid transparent;
    transform: translateX(-50%);
  }

  .bubble-box::before {
    border-bottom: 7px solid var(--color-border);
  }

  .bubble-box::after {
    top: -6px;
    border-right-width: 6px;
    border-bottom: 6px solid var(--color-bg-secondary);
    border-left-width: 6px;
  }

  .bubble-num {
    display: flex;
    flex: none; /* fixed circle, never shrunk to make room for the text */
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    width: 1.1rem;
    height: 1.1rem;
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.7rem;
    font-weight: var(--fw-semibold);
    color: var(--color-bg);
    background: var(--color-accent);
    border-radius: 50%;
  }

  /* The text half of the same flex row — `min-width: 0` is the standard
     flexbox fix that lets a text item shrink below its own unbroken content
     width and actually wrap, instead of overflowing `.bubble-box`. */
  .bubble-text {
    flex: 1 1 auto;
    min-width: 0;
  }

  /* ===== Bubble cycle ===========================================================
     A 30s loop, shaped by six DWELLS (11% each, 3.3s — one per bubble/
     stage, including "destination" since the 2026-08-23 destination-hop
     update) separated by six TRAVEL windows: five short 3%-wide hops
     between adjacent stages (11–14%, 25–28%, 39–42%, 53–56%, 67–70%) and
     one long 19%-wide RETURN hop (81–100%) that carries the highlight box
     back from "destination" to "client" to restart the loop. These are the
     exact same breakpoints `box-travel` below uses for the highlight box's
     `left`/`width` — this is what keeps a bubble's text and the box
     arriving at its stage in lockstep, with one shared timeline (30s, same
     start, same `infinite`) instead of two clocks that could drift. Adding
     the sixth dwell+hop shrank the other five dwells (14%→11%) and short
     hops (3% stayed 3%, unchanged) to keep the loop at 30s rather than
     stretching it — see this file's header comment, "UPDATE 2026-08-23
     (destination hop)".

     Each bubble fades in/out over a 3%-wide window (matching the short
     forward hops) positioned at the edges of its own dwell — bubble 1 both
     opens the loop (0–11%) AND closes it (fading in over the last 3% of the
     long return hop, 97–100%, so its 100% value already equals its 0%
     value: a genuinely seamless loop, not a hard cut). Bubble 6 mirrors
     that at the other end: it fades out over the FIRST 3% of the return
     hop (81–84%) and then stays invisible for the rest of that long hop —
     the box is still visibly travelling during that stretch, just with no
     bubble narrating it, which reads as a brief beat before the loop
     restarts rather than a jump cut. (This "fade early, stay dark through
     the long hop" role moved from bubble 5 to bubble 6 with the
     destination-hop update — bubble 5 is now a normal middle-of-the-loop
     bubble like 2/3/4, fading in and out over its own two adjacent short
     hops.) */
  @keyframes phase-1 {
    0%,
    11% {
      opacity: 100%;
    }

    14%,
    84% {
      opacity: 0%;
    }

    /* 84→87%: the same window the box uses to fade back in at stage 1, so the
       caption and the highlight reappear together. It used to wait until 97%,
       which was fine while the box spent that time crawling backwards across
       the diagram; with the crawl gone it was just four seconds of nothing. */
    87%,
    100% {
      opacity: 100%;
    }
  }

  @keyframes phase-2 {
    0%,
    11%,
    100% {
      opacity: 0%;
    }

    14%,
    25% {
      opacity: 100%;
    }

    28% {
      opacity: 0%;
    }
  }

  @keyframes phase-3 {
    0%,
    25%,
    100% {
      opacity: 0%;
    }

    28%,
    39% {
      opacity: 100%;
    }

    42% {
      opacity: 0%;
    }
  }

  @keyframes phase-4 {
    0%,
    39%,
    100% {
      opacity: 0%;
    }

    42%,
    53% {
      opacity: 100%;
    }

    56% {
      opacity: 0%;
    }
  }

  @keyframes phase-5 {
    0%,
    53%,
    100% {
      opacity: 0%;
    }

    56%,
    67% {
      opacity: 100%;
    }

    70% {
      opacity: 0%;
    }
  }

  @keyframes phase-6 {
    0%,
    67%,
    100% {
      opacity: 0%;
    }

    70%,
    81% {
      opacity: 100%;
    }

    84% {
      opacity: 0%;
    }
  }

  /* ===== Traveling highlight box: the `box-travel` animation ===================

     GENERATED, not hand-written. The numbers come from `.mcp-strip`'s
     `grid-template-columns` — today `0.65fr 1.5fr 0.9fr 1.225fr 1.225fr 1.5fr`,
     7fr in total — this way, and any change to the columns forces all three to
     be redone together (these stops, `COL_X` in the frontmatter, and the
     bubbles' `--pos`):

       edge_i   = (sum of the preceding widths) / total
       centre_i = edge_i + width_i / 2
       box_i    = max(width_i × 0.90, label_i + 4px), capped at the column width

     That last line is why the boxes do NOT match their column's width. They
     narrow by 10% so the highlight hugs the stage rather than its column —
     except at Cloudflare and destination, where the floor wins: at 360px their
     labels (50px and 55px, measured in the browser, not estimated) already fill
     the column, and shrinking them would clip the text.

     The column split is not free either: the gap between two stages is the mean
     of their widths, so pulling nodes away from egress means taking it from
     somewhere else. It was taken from the first two runs (user→Cloudflare and
     Cloudflare→nginx), which say nothing that needs the room, and nodes→egress
     went from 14.286% to 17.5% of the strip.

     Each stop lasts 14% of the cycle (11% still, 3% travelling across three
     sub-runs: ease-in / linear / ease-out), and the long return at the end
     (81–100%) is done with opacity instead of a journey back: crossing the
     whole figure backwards read as a seventh step that does not exist. */
  @keyframes box-travel {
    0% {
      left: 0.464%;
      width: 8.357%;
      opacity: 100%;
    }

    11% {
      left: 0.464%;
      width: 8.357%;
      animation-timing-function: ease-in;
    }

    12% {
      left: 3.762%;
      width: 12%;
      animation-timing-function: linear;
    }

    13% {
      left: 7.06%;
      width: 15.643%;
      animation-timing-function: ease-out;
    }

    14% {
      left: 10.357%;
      width: 19.286%;
    }

    25% {
      left: 10.357%;
      width: 19.286%;
      animation-timing-function: ease-in;
    }

    26% {
      left: 17.357%;
      width: 16.714%;
      animation-timing-function: linear;
    }

    27% {
      left: 24.357%;
      width: 14.143%;
      animation-timing-function: ease-out;
    }

    28% {
      left: 31.357%;
      width: 11.571%;
    }

    39% {
      left: 31.357%;
      width: 11.571%;
      animation-timing-function: ease-in;
    }

    40% {
      left: 35.72%;
      width: 12.964%;
      animation-timing-function: linear;
    }

    41% {
      left: 40.083%;
      width: 14.357%;
      animation-timing-function: ease-out;
    }

    42% {
      left: 44.446%;
      width: 15.75%;
    }

    53% {
      left: 44.446%;
      width: 15.75%;
      animation-timing-function: ease-in;
    }

    54% {
      left: 50.28%;
      width: 15.75%;
      animation-timing-function: linear;
    }

    55% {
      left: 56.113%;
      width: 15.75%;
      animation-timing-function: ease-out;
    }

    56% {
      left: 61.946%;
      width: 15.75%;
    }

    67% {
      left: 61.946%;
      width: 15.75%;
      animation-timing-function: ease-in;
    }

    68% {
      left: 67.731%;
      width: 17.158%;
      animation-timing-function: linear;
    }

    69% {
      left: 73.515%;
      width: 18.566%;
      animation-timing-function: ease-out;
    }

    70% {
      left: 79.299%;
      width: 19.973%;
    }

    81% {
      left: 79.299%;
      width: 19.973%;
      opacity: 100%;
    }

    84% {
      left: 79.299%;
      width: 19.973%;
      opacity: 0%;
    }

    84.01% {
      left: 0.464%;
      width: 8.357%;
      opacity: 0%;
    }

    87% {
      left: 0.464%;
      width: 8.357%;
      opacity: 100%;
    }

    100% {
      left: 0.464%;
      width: 8.357%;
      opacity: 100%;
    }
  }

  /* ===== prefers-reduced-motion =================================================
     `base.css` already forces `animation-duration: 0.001ms !important`
     globally, but that alone would freeze every bubble and the highlight
     box on whatever frame the style recalculation happened to land on —
     not necessarily a coherent one. The rules below follow the same
     pattern as `.realtime-pill__dot` in utilities.css: turn the animations
     off explicitly, so a single coherent base state wins for all of them
     at once — no bubbles at all, no highlight box travelling. In their
     place, ONE static ring appears around the illustrative "always this
     node" match (`MATCHED_NODE_INDEX` in the frontmatter above): the
     frozen frame the spec asks for. The prose timeline right below the
     figure already carries every word the bubbles would have. */
  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    .bubble {
      display: none;
    }

    /* The current is motion and nothing else: frozen it would only speckle
       the wires, so it goes away entirely and they stay solid. */
    .flow {
      display: none;
    }

    .mcp-box-fill,
    .mcp-box-border {
      display: none;
    }

    /* Both rings — the node and ITS exit — because the frozen frame's whole
       message is "the same client lands on the same node, and that is why
       its exit is stable": a ring on the node alone showed half of it. Both
       are ILLUSTRATIVE (see `MATCHED_NODE_INDEX`). */
    .node-badge--matched .node-badge-num,
    .stage-icon--egress-matched {
      background: var(--accent-dim);
      border-color: var(--color-accent);
      border-width: 2.5px;
      box-shadow: 0 0 0 2px var(--accent-dim);
    }
  }

  /* ===== Responsive =============================================================
     The strip stays on the same 8-column grid at every width — see this
     block's header comment for why that alone keeps it horizontal with no
     reflow. Below 480px, labels and badges shrink a little further and the
     bubble zone grows a little taller, both because the narrower columns
     wrap short labels ("Cloudflare", "destination") onto more lines than at
     rest — verified rendered at 360px, see the SDD report. */
  @media (width <= 480px) {
    /* A smaller `.stage-label` and a hand-shrunk `.node-badge-num` used to live
       here so the long labels would fit below 480px. With the figure scaling as
       one block they are unnecessary: shrinking them again would break the
       proportion exactly where it has just been fixed. */

    .mcp-bubbles {
      height: 5.75rem;
    }

    .bubble-box {
      font-size: 0.78rem;
    }
  }

  /* ===== Caption ================================================================
     `margin-top` only, not `.mcp-bubbles`'s own reserved height (see "The
     fixed-height bubble zone" comment above — that height stays fixed on
     purpose, it is what keeps the page from jumping). The author wondered
     whether, on desktop, there was too much empty space between the
     information bubble and the animation box's footer — measured before
     this change, the gap between `.mcp-bubbles`'s bottom edge and this
     caption's top edge was exactly this margin (0.85rem = 13.6px at
     1280px, 12.24px at 360px, since nothing else sits between them).
     Halved to 0.4rem: still a visible break between the figure and its
     caption, but not the fixed zone's own reserved whitespace below a
     short bubble, which this rule cannot and should not touch. */
  .mcp-flow-caption {
    max-width: 62ch;
    margin: 0.4rem 0 0;
    font-size: 0.8125rem;
    color: var(--color-text-muted);
    text-wrap: pretty;
  }

  /* ===== Prose timeline =========================================================
     Real HTML, same six numbers as the bubbles above — this is what a
     crawler, an assistant reading this page, or a `prefers-reduced-motion`
     visitor actually reads; see this file's header comment for why the
     detail lives here and not in the figure. Numbers are visual only
     (`.mcp-timeline-num`, `aria-hidden`) — the native `<ol>` already gives
     assistive tech "item N of 6" for free, which is why `list-style` is
